TI  - LIPOPROTEIN-RELATED CORONARY RISK-FACTORS IN PATIENTS WITH ANGIOGRAPHICALLY DEFINED CORONARY-ARTERY DISEASE AND CONTROLS - IMPROVED GROUP SEPARATION BY INDEXES REFLECTING THE BALANCE BETWEEN LOW-DENSITY AND HIGH-DENSITY-LIPOPROTEINS
T2  - CORONARY ARTERY DISEASE
AB  - To elucidate the extent to which some common coronary risk factors and ratios estimating the balance between low- and high-density lipoproteins (LDL and HDL) might help distinguish between coronary patients and controls, serum total cholesterol (TC), HDL cholesterol (HDL(c)), apolipoprotein A (apo A) and apolipoprotein B (apo B) levels were determined in 46 patients with angiographically proven coronary artery disease (group CAD), 52 subjects with normal coronary angiogram (group C), and 53 healthy subjects attending a health screening service (group K).  Lipoprotein levels in CAD patients were different from those in controls.  In groups CAD, C, and K, respectively, mean values +/- standard error of the mean were:  for TC 7.2 +/- 0.2, 5.4 +/- 0.2, and 6.3 +/- 0.2 mM; for apo B 156 +/- 5, 125 +/- 4, and 121 +/- 4 mg/dL; for HDL(c) 0.77 +/- 0.02, 1.07 +/- 0.04, and 1.29 +/- 0.04 mM; for apo A 182 +/- 3, 219 +/- 6, and 244 +/- 5 mg/dL; for HDL-2 (activity) 0.95 +/- 0.03, 0.94 +/- 0.03, and 1.14 +/- 0.02; for apo B/apo A ratio 0.87 +/- 0.03, 0.58 +/- 0.02, and 0.50 +/- 0.02; for TC/HDL(c) ratio 9.82 +/- 0.49, 5.33 +/- 0.25, and 5.02 +/- 0.19; for (TC-HDL(c))/HDL(c) 8.82 +/- 0.49, 4.33 +/- 0.25, and 4.02 +/- 0.19.  The best discrimination was obtained using an atherogenic index:  ATH-INDEX = [TC-HDL(c)] x [apo B]/[apo A] x [HDL(c)].  Values for this index in the CAD, C, and K groups, respectively, were 8.3 +/- 0.9, 2.7 +/- 0.2, and 2.2 +/- 0.2.  The results confirm that LDL/HDL indexes may be more useful than individual lipoprotein components in distinguishing between CAD patients and controls.
