TI  - DISTRIBUTION OF SERUM LIPIDS IN TUBERCULOUS PATIENTS AND ITS RELATION TO CLINICAL CORONARY HEART DISEASE
T2  - AMERICAN JOURNAL OF CLINICAL PATHOLOGY
AB  - Tuberculous patients as a group manifest a mean beta-alpha lipoprotein ratio that is significantly less than that found in control groups. This low atherogenic index is consistent with their low incidence of clinical coronary heart disease. Two types of contradiction to laboratory findings are noted: (1) 4 instances of abnormally high beta-alpha lipoprotein ratio, of which 1 case subsequently confirmed the laboratory findings with a myocardial infarction; and (2) 2 instances in which positive autopsy findings were contrary to the atherogenic index. A mechanism is suggested wherein pulmonary tuberculosis may operate to protect against coronary atherosclerosis and thrombosis in a manner similar to that of hepatic disease, in contrast to other conditions, such as diabetes, which favor coronary heart disease.
