TI  - SERUM PARAMETERS AS DISCRIMINATORS BETWEEN NORMAL AND CORONARY GROUPS
T2  - CIRCULATION
AB  - Several serum parameters were evaluated as discriminators between a normal (N) and a coronary disease (MI) group. The best discriminating parameters were Sof 0-400 lipo-protein concentrations and the calculated atherogenic index. The means of the N and MI groups for each lipoprotein class were significantly separated. Total cholesterol was a less effective discriminator than total [beta]-lipoprotein concentration or atherogenic index, but more effective than [beta]-cholesterol or lipid phosphorus. The data indicate that (a) some importance must be ascribed to the protein components of the lipoprotein moieties, and (b) a re-evaluation of the weighting factors in the calculated atherogenic index might lead to a more sensitive parameter.
