TI  - NEWER CLINICAL AND LABORATORY STUDIES IN THE AGED .5. LIPIDOGRAM BY PAPER ELECTROPHORESIS IN NORMAL PATIENTS 80-100 YEARS OF AGE - A PRELIMINARY REPORT
T2  - AMERICAN JOURNAL OF DIGESTIVE DISEASES
AB  - The present report endeavors to ascertain, for the first time, any correlation between paper electrophoretic and ultracentrifugal methods of serum lipids in normal patients 80-100 years of age. Two groups of patients were studied - group A (old age group) consisted of "normal " geriatic patients from 80-94, with an average age of 90 years. Group B (younger age group) ranged in age from 17 to 46, with an average of 33 years. The old age group had no metabolic, hepatic, nor coronary artery disease, but one of generalized arteriosclerosis. The younger age group had no cardio-vascular nor coronary artery disease. They were post-operative or psychoneurotic individuals and were studied for comparative purposes. Serum lipid partitions, standard Sf lipoprotein molecules, and atherogenic index were found to be low in normal patients, age 80-100 years. A correlation between paper electrophoretic and ultracentrifugal methods of serum lipids was observed. The protein distribution shows a lower albumin and A/G ratio; and a higher beta globulin value in the old age group than in the young. There was no significant difference between the lipoprotein distribution in the 2 age groups examined. Abnormal lipidograms, showing increase in the "beta" lipoprotein group - were found in only 2 patients from the old age group and 2 patients from the younger age group. The increased "beta" - lipoprotein values did not occur in these old age group patients having increased Sf lipoprotein molecules and increased serum lipid partitions. There was no correlation between the chemical serum lipid partitions; Sf - lipoprotein molecules by the ultracentrifugal method; and the lipid distribution diagrams (lipidogram) by paper electrophoresis.
