TI  - LONG-TERM EFFICACY AND SAFETY OF CIPROFIBRATE IN PATIENTS WITH PRIMARY HYPERLIPIDEMIA
T2  - CURRENT THERAPEUTIC RESEARCH-CLINICAL AND EXPERIMENTAL
AB  - Ciprofibrate was studied for long-term efficacy in 102 patients with diet-resistant primary hyperlipidemia in an open study. Dosages of 100 or 200 mg once daily were based on clinical judgment and treatment continued for up to 12 years, with a mean duration of 6.7 years. Ciprofibrate effectively lowered total and low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol, atherogenic index, and total and very-low-density triglycerides in all types of hyperlipidemia (IIA, IIB, III, and IV). These effects persisted during long-term treatment. There were only three possible drug-related withdrawals due to adverse events and these were considered not to be of a serious nature. No clinically significant, drug-induced, abnormal laboratory changes were found. The plasma ciprofibrate concentration reached a steady state level within approximately one month and remained stable thereafter. Ciprofibrate is a safe drug with good compliance suitable for long-term treatment of different types of primary hyperlipidemia. The recommended standard dose is 100 mg daily, but to achieve an optimal effect, especially in patients with elevated LDL cholesterol, 200 mg once daily can be safety given.
